  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 130 PM MDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nMuch cooler Tue behind the front, especially across the plains\nwhere highs will run some 15-20 degf lower than Mon. Less \ndramatic cooling over the mountains, were temps will drift \ndownward only a few degrees. Air mass on the plains will\ninitially be too cool/stable for much if any convection during \nthe daytime hours Tue, while over the mountains, showers and \nstorms will become widespread, especially along the Continental \nDivide where mid-level moisture will continue to increase. Some \nweakening convection may reach the I-25 Corridor Tue night as \nstorms drift east off the higher terrain, and  portions of the \nplains may see t-storms overnight, as nocturnal convection along\nthe old frontal boundary over nern NM moves northward in \nresponse to strengthening low level jet. \n\nStill appears strong monsoonal moisture tap develops Wed-Fri, \nwith trough along the West Coast and upper high over the \nsoutheastern U.S. Showers/thunderstorms will be widespread \nacross the area each afternoon/evening through the period, with \nthe heaviest rainfall over the Continental Divide (especially \nswrn mts), while the far eastern plains will see lighter \namounts/lower pops. Burn scar flash flood will increase Wed-Fri,\nthough heaviest rainfall may stay west of the Aspen Acres scar\nif model QPF forecasts are correct. May start to see some \ndrying over the wrn half of the area Sat, with drier air \nspreading eastward Sun/Mon, though model spread becomes rather \nwide over the weekend into next week, and at least a few \nmodels/ensemble members keep daily precip going into early next \nweek. Temps will drift back back upward a few degf Wed-Fri, \nbefore potential cold front drops readings back below average \nfor Sat/Sun.",
